insonic maintained WebView lifetime, callback and native-key patch

Upstream: github.com/wailsapp/go-webview2 v1.0.23
https://github.com/wailsapp/go-webview2/tree/v1.0.23

Original upstream files and MIT license are retained. The insonic additions
use Apache-2.0 (LICENSE.insonic). A native parent-window lifetime subclass
closes the WebView controller once on its owning UI thread during destruction.
The ordinary window procedure continues normally.

The insonic source tree contains third_party/go-webview2.source.json with
exact upstream and maintained source checksums. The main product source
revision binds this repository-local replacement; Go binary metadata records
the relative replacement path rather than an upstream checksum for the patch.

The maintained cleanup also balances owned COM interface references and the original UI-thread apartment initialization. The manifest lists all modified upstream files and every added file.

The native system-key extension copies original accelerator context on the
owned window UI thread. Claimed keys retain one handled disposition; the host
posts native menu work instead of directly entering modal UI in the callback. Other
accelerators retain their existing callback route.

While an owned native menu is active, the parent retains keyboard focus and
does not forward WM_SETFOCUS back into the browser. Menu exit restores the
previous owned child only while the same parent still owns foreground input.

Explicit disposable qualification can retain fixed navigation/resource callback
counts and the browser version for bounded failure evidence. It does not retain
request URLs, script contents or application data.

The resource deferral Release method dispatches to IUnknown.Release, balancing
the acquired reference after completion instead of increasing its count.

Disposable qualification also observes native response assignment, header-interface
acquisition, status/reason, content publication and deferral completion. Individual
AppendHeader calls and browser consumption are not observed. Fixed
entered/returned/HRESULT/thread counters contain no request content or native
addresses. Internal borrowed identities are retired with their corresponding
acquisition/Release and window owner. The response-call observer adds no WebView2 calls; observation reads native thread identity.
The runtime_version diagnostic field remains an available-loader lookup, not
independently verified created-environment identity.

The nine active Chromium callbacks use the existing native-allocated combridge
with canonical IUnknown identity, supported-interface queries and real retained
references. Creator leases are allocated only for an attached owned window and
closed after controller/interface cleanup. Late events are ignored; a late
controller is closed rather than installed into a destroyed owner. The default
Go loader captures its completion target before asynchronous scheduling.

Accelerator registration passes the caller-owned EventRegistrationToken output
pointer at its native ABI level, preserving registration and HRESULT behavior.

Eight fixed active-request slots retain unique publication/completion and sticky
failed-call totals across initial responses. Slot overflow and retirement remain
explicit. Only the exact runtime-ready message receives a fixed counter, with
existing message forwarding unchanged and no message content retained.

Disposable qualification now queries BrowserVersionString exactly once through the
created environment completion callback, records creation/getter HRESULT and
explicit available versus actual version identity, and frees the returned string.
This passive observation adds no CDP setup or navigation acknowledgement gate.
Ordinary application launches do not elect this getter.

A separately elected disposable browser observer reuses that created-environment
version and registers seven CDP receivers with four acknowledged setup commands
before diagnostic navigation. It retains only bounded fixed asset classes, numeric
fetch/header/exception summaries and matching document loader/frame milestones.
Receivers and callback leases close on the owning thread. Ordinary launches
add no observer COM calls; observed fetch completion is not script execution.

SDK convenience calls balance internally acquired settings and queried controller
references on success and failure. Public getter outputs remain caller-owned.
Native query errors are preserved and null successful outputs are rejected. The
background-colour temporary is released before its existing fatal error callback.

Startup now returns the first synchronous or asynchronous environment/controller
failure instead of terminating the process or leaving initialization pending.
Failed and null outputs, interrupted message waits, startup process failures and
bootstrap-request rejection cannot publish successful embedding. Generated
startup boundaries retain original native causes through Go error wrapping.

Reentrant destruction marks startup cancelled and defers destructive controller,
interface and callback-lease cleanup until completion callbacks and synchronous
native startup/configuration calls unwind. Caller-owned windows still follow
normal destruction, and a fresh Chromium/process can use its intended profile.
The existing bool Embed API remains available. Error-returning startup facades
reuse the architecture-specific SDK calls without changing their native ABI.

The COM balance regression uses a fresh native thread and observes its actual
apartment transition, including an implicit MTA, rather than assuming a reused
Go worker thread has no apartment. Ordinary browser/document readiness and
native qualification operation allowances remain separate and unchanged.
